@page "/guides/rolling-out-epegboard-to-your-club"
@using PegboardWebSite.Services
@{
    Layout = "_LayoutMarketing";
    ViewData["Title"] = "How to roll out ePegboard to your club";
    ViewData["Description"] = "A before / on-the-night / after playbook for switching your club night to ePegboard so it lands with your players and keeps them coming back.";
    ViewData["OgTitle"] = "Rolling out ePegboard to your club";

    const string published = "2026-08-02";
    const string canonical = "https://www.epegboard.com/guides/rolling-out-epegboard-to-your-club";

    var faqPairs = new (string Q, string A)[]
    {
        ("What is the best way to introduce ePegboard to a club night?",
         "Run a simple loop and repeat it every session. Before the night, tell the club why you are switching and tie it to a problem members already grumble about, then explain how it will work. On the night, have the tablet out somewhere people gather so they can see it running. After the night, share the session recap link and point out that claiming their profile unlocks their own stats from that evening. The single most important part is that last step: lead with what a player gets to see, not with a request to sign up."),
        ("Do players have to create an account to play?",
         "No. The organiser runs the night and adds players at the tablet, and nobody needs an account for that. Claiming a profile is entirely optional and happens after the session, off the back of the recap link. What it adds is the personal side: a player's own results, rating and history from that night and every night after. So the club night works for everyone from game one, and accounts are the bonus for players who want to follow their own progress."),
        ("How do I help my players get the most out of ePegboard?",
         "Point them beyond the recap. The recap of the night is only the first taste - claiming a profile is what opens up the rest for a player: their own rating and how it moves, their game history at your club, their progress over the weeks. That is what keeps the night giving them something after they have packed up and gone home. Your job is not to chase anyone into signing up; it is simply to show them what is there. Share the session recap and mention that claiming their profile lets them see their own games and rating from the night, then let curiosity do the rest. Sharing it with a few keen members first helps, so when it reaches the wider group chat it reads as look what you can see rather than a request to register."),
        ("Why should I explain the switch before anyone sees the app?",
         "Because a change that answers a complaint people already have is welcomed, and a change that appears out of nowhere is resisted. If members have grumbled that games do not rotate, or that court time feels uneven, name that problem when you announce the switch and say this is what fixes it. You are not selling an app; you are solving something the club already feels. That framing is what turns a new tablet on the table from an intrusion into something people are curious to try."),
        ("How quickly will players start engaging?",
         "Faster than most organisers expect, if you run the loop consistently. One club that ran this loop consistently had more than twenty players on their own accounts after just two sessions, where most clubs see a handful trickle in over weeks. The difference was not the club being unusually keen; it was the method: explain the why beforehand, keep the tablet accessible, and after the night share the recap leading with the personal stats. Do that every session and it compounds."),
        ("Do we have to let ePegboard pick every game?",
         "No - you choose how hands-on to be. You can let it build every game and just tap start, review and tweak each suggestion before starting, or run it pegboard-style: turn on 'Force include first queue player' and 'Limit to first N players in queue', then hand the tablet to the player at the front of the queue to pick their own game from the next few waiting and swap it around if they want. Plenty of clubs coming from a pegboard start in that traditional style - players enjoy the sense of control - and move toward fully automated over a few weeks as they see the balanced games it makes. Running the shared tablet in kiosk mode lets you pass it safely between players while admin controls stay behind your organiser PIN."),
        ("How should we set players' starting ratings when we begin?",
         "You can leave everyone on the default and let the system learn them, but games in the first few sessions will be more hit-and-miss. Better is to give each member an approximate rating when you add them - a rough tier is plenty - which you can set on import or on the member form. Even loose guesses make the opening games noticeably closer from the very first night."),
        ("The games look a bit unbalanced in our first few weeks - is something wrong?",
         "No, that is normal while ratings settle. A new player's rating swings around for their first couple of dozen games before it settles down. Keep an eye on the suggestions during that period and override any that look lopsided - swap a player, or build the game by hand. It does not slow the system down: ratings are learned from the game results, not from who picked the game, so they still hone in on the right numbers underneath.")
    };

    var articleJsonLd = GuideSchema.Article(
        "How to roll out ePegboard to your club",
        "A before / on-the-night / after playbook for switching your club night to ePegboard so it lands with your players and keeps them coming back.",
        canonical, published);
    var faqJsonLd = GuideSchema.Faq(faqPairs);
}

@section Head {
    <script type="application/ld+json">@Html.Raw(articleJsonLd)</script>
    <script type="application/ld+json">@Html.Raw(faqJsonLd)</script>
}

