      <h1 style="margin-top:10px">How to roll out ePegboard to your club</h1>
      <p class="lede">Deciding to use an app is the easy part. Getting a club to take to it - and getting your players to really engage with it - is where organisers get stuck. This is the loop one club used to do exactly that, and why each step works.</p>
      <div class="guide-meta">
        <span class="gm-tag">Guide</span>
        <span>Adoption</span>
        <span>10 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">
    <div class="guide-toc">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#loop">The loop at a glance</a>
      <a href="#problem">Start from a problem your members already feel</a>
      <a href="#before">Before the session: tell the club why</a>
      <a href="#night">On the night: put the tablet where people gather</a>
      <a href="#after">After the session: share the recap, lead with the stats</a>
      <a href="#repeat">Repeat it, identically</a>
      <a href="#pace">Ease in at your own pace: how much you let the app decide</a>
      <a href="#ratings">Give the ratings a good start</a>
      <a href="#questions">Answer the questions your members will ask</a>
      <a href="#help">If you get stuck, we are one email away</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">
      <p class="lead">Most organisers who move their club night to an app expect the hard part to be the software. It rarely is. The app runs the night from evening one. The part that decides whether the move sticks is the people: whether the club takes to it, and whether your players actually engage with it rather than the whole thing living on one organiser's tablet. Getting players onto their own accounts is a big part of that - it is what lets the night keep giving them something once they have gone home, which is what keeps them coming back - and it is the piece most organisers find hardest. This guide is the playbook for exactly that, drawn from one club that ran it consistently and got more than twenty players onto their own accounts in just two sessions, where most clubs see a handful trickle in over weeks. Here is precisely what they did, and why each step pulls its weight.</p>

      <h2 id="loop">The loop at a glance</h2>
      <p>The whole thing is one short loop, run the same way every session. Nothing here is clever; the power is in doing it consistently rather than once.</p>
      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>The before / on-the-night / after loop</h4>
        <ol style="margin:8px 0 0;padding-left:20px">
          <li><strong>Before</strong> - post in your club chat: why you are switching (tied to a real gripe) and how it will work.</li>
          <li><strong>On the night</strong> - have the tablet out and accessible; let people be curious.</li>
          <li><strong>After</strong> - share the session recap and lead with what players can see: their own stats when they claim their profile.</li>
          <li><strong>Repeat</strong> - run the same three steps next session, and the next.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <h2 id="problem">Start from a problem your members already feel</h2>
      <p>The clubs that adopt smoothly do not introduce an app because it is new; they introduce it because it fixes something members were already complaining about. One club on ePegboard switched because the traditional pegboard was not rotating games well, and that was a genuine, named grievance in the club before any app appeared. When the change is the answer to a gripe people already have, it is welcomed. When it appears out of nowhere, it is resisted.</p>
      <p>So before you announce anything, be clear in your own head what problem you are solving. Uneven court time, the same four always playing together, games that are never quite balanced, no record of who played whom - these are the usual ones, and each is something our guides on <a href="/guides/fair-court-time-busy-club-night">fair court time</a>, <a href="/guides/game-variety-stop-same-players">game variety</a> and <a href="/guides/badminton-club-night-rotation">rotation</a> dig into. Pick the one your members actually feel, and lead with that.</p>

      <h2 id="before">Before the session: tell the club why</h2>
      <p>A day or two before the first night, post in whatever channel your club already lives in - a WhatsApp group, a Facebook group, wherever the chatter happens. Two things, kept short:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Why we are changing.</strong> Name the problem from the section above. We have all felt the rotation going stale, so we are trying something that sorts it. This does the heavy lifting: it frames the tablet as a fix, not a fad.</li>
        <li><strong>How it will work.</strong> A sentence on what to expect on the night - there will be a tablet, you will still just turn up and play, the app decides the games. No homework for members, nothing to download before they arrive.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>You are not trying to explain the whole app. You are removing the surprise, so that when people walk in and see a tablet on the table it is the thing they were told about, not an unexplained change to how their club night runs. Not sure how to word it? Our <a href="https://docs.epegboard.com/how-to/organiser-message-templates/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">organiser message templates</a> give you a copy-paste version of each of these messages to tweak for your club.</p>

      <h2 id="night">On the night: put the tablet where people gather</h2>
      <p>On the first night, have the tablet down and accessible - somewhere people naturally stand and wait, not tucked away by the organiser. That is genuinely most of the work. Players get curious about a screen showing courts, names and who is up next, and they wander over and look. You do not need a launch speech. A visible, running board that people can see doing its job sells itself far better than any announcement.</p>
      <p>Run the night as normal. The point of evening one is not to get anyone signing up; it is simply to let the club see the thing work and feel that it is fair. Everything after this step is easier because people have now watched it run.</p>

      <h2 id="after">After the session: share the recap, lead with the stats</h2>
      <p>This is the step that matters most, and the one most clubs get slightly wrong. When the session ends, ePegboard gives you a shareable recap of the night. Here is how the best club used it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Test it with a few people first.</strong> Send the recap link to a handful of keen members before anyone else. When they have opened it and had a look, you are ready for the wider post.</li>
        <li><strong>Then post it to the whole club</strong> - and lead with what they can see, not with a request. Something like: here is tonight's session, and if you claim your profile you can see your own games, results and rating from the night. That framing - look what you can see - works far better than please go and make an account.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The reason the pre-share matters is subtle but real: by the time the link hits the main group, a few people have already engaged with it, so it lands as something worth opening rather than a cold ask. And leading with the player's own stats is the actual hook. Claiming a profile is optional and happens off the back of curiosity about their own results - which is a far stronger pull than being told to register. This is the step that turns a night run on one organiser's tablet into a club whose players are engaged with it in their own right. There is a <a href="https://docs.epegboard.com/how-to/organiser-message-templates/#after-the-session" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ready-made after-session message</a> you can copy and drop your recap link into.</p>

      <div class="cmp-wrap">
        <table class="cmp-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Instead of</th><th>Do this</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Announcing an app because it is new</td><td>Frame it as the fix for a gripe members already have</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Springing the tablet on people cold</td><td>Explain the why and how in the club chat first</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A launch speech on the night</td><td>Put the tablet where people gather and let curiosity work</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Please go and sign up</td><td>Claim your profile to see your own stats from tonight</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Posting the recap cold to everyone</td><td>Share with a few first, then post to the wider club</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="repeat">Repeat it, identically</h2>
      <p>None of the above is a one-off launch. The club in this example ran the same loop for their second session and every session after: a message before to say how they were using it, the tablet accessible on the night, the recap shared afterwards leading with the stats. Consistency is what compounds. Each session a few more players claim their profile, the recaps get more familiar, and within a handful of weeks it is simply how the club night runs. Treat it as a habit, not a launch event.</p>

      <h2 id="pace">Ease in at your own pace: how much you let the app decide</h2>
      <p>You do not have to hand the whole night over to the app on day one. It runs in three broad styles, and a club used to a pegboard can start at the gentle end and move along the scale as trust builds.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fully automated.</strong> The picker builds each game and you just tap Start. Best once the club trusts it - and for this you want to leave the picking constraints off, so the picker has the freedom to make the fairest, most varied games.</li>
        <li><strong>Organiser-led.</strong> The picker suggests each game and you review it - swap a player, lock someone in, change the format - before you start it. The hands-on middle ground, and where most organisers begin.</li>
        <li><strong>Traditional, pegboard-style.</strong> The closest thing to the board people already know. In the session settings, turn on <strong>Force include first queue player</strong> and <strong>Limit to first N players in queue</strong>, then hand the tablet to the player at the front of the queue and let them pick their own game from the next few waiting - and swap the suggestion around if they fancy different players. It is assisted picking: just like taking the top peg and choosing your four, except the app keeps it fair and keeps the record.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>That last style is worth calling out on its own. Clubs that let the front player pick their game and swap the suggestions around have found it genuinely popular - it gives players a sense of control and makes the wait part of the fun, rather than something the organiser does to them. Plenty of pegboard clubs start here and drift toward automated over a few weeks, once people have seen the balanced games it makes.</p>
      <p>A device split that works nicely: run the shared tablet in <a href="https://docs.epegboard.com/club-nights/kiosk-mode/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kiosk mode</a> so it can be passed safely between players - they can check people in, run the games and enter scores, while the admin controls stay locked behind your organiser PIN. Keep the jobs that need sign-off - manual overrides, voiding a game, changing settings - on your own phone, which steps into admin mode with the same PIN. The tablet lives with the players; you keep the few controls that need a grown-up, without hovering over the desk.</p>
      <p>And players do not have to crowd the tablet at all. Anyone can follow the night live from their own phone by typing the code shown on the club's screen - the live board, who is on, who is up next - and once they are checked in they get their own running total for the evening, updating as each game finishes. No account is needed to follow along; claiming a profile is what turns that into a rating and history they keep. It is a good thing to mention in your after-the-session post - see <a href="/for-players">the player side</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="ratings">Give the ratings a good start</h2>
      <p>ePegboard builds its balanced games from player ratings, so the better your starting ratings, the better the games from the very first night.</p>
      <p>You can leave everyone on the default - new players start at a middle rating - and let the system work each person out. It will. But the games in your first few sessions will be more hit-and-miss while it does, because it is starting with no idea who is who.</p>
      <p>Better is to give each member an approximate rating when you add them - a rough tier is plenty (a beginner, an improver, a solid club player, a strong county-level player). You can set it on import or on the member form. Even loose guesses make the opening games noticeably closer and save the system a lot of guessing.</p>
      <p>Either way, expect movement early. A new player's rating swings around a fair bit for their first couple of dozen games while the system learns where they sit, then it settles down. During that settling-in period, keep half an eye on the suggestions: if one comes out lopsided, override it - swap a player, or build the game by hand.</p>
      <p>Here is the reassuring part. Doing that does not slow the system down or throw it off. It learns each player's level from the game <em>results</em>, not from who chose the game - so overriding a pick while ratings are still settling simply smooths the games on the night; underneath, the ratings still hone in on the right numbers. You are helping the night along, not fighting the system. For the detail, see <a href="https://docs.epegboard.com/ratings/overview/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">how ratings work</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="questions">Answer the questions your members will ask</h2>
      <p>A few questions come up almost every time, and having answers ready keeps momentum. Members tend to ask how it works, why it is free, and whether it will keep being improved. The easiest move is to do a little homework and point people at the material that already answers them, rather than fielding each one yourself:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>How does it work?</strong> The <a href="/guides">guides section</a> and the <a href="/features/club-nights">club nights feature page</a> explain what the app does and why games come out the way they do.</li>
        <li><strong>Why is it free?</strong> The more clubs use ePegboard, the better it gets for everyone - more inter-club play, more feedback, more improvements - so growing the community matters to us more than charging for the club night. It is free for clubs, with no card and no paywall on the basics. Point members at the <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a> for the fuller answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Will it keep being updated?</strong> Yes - it is actively developed and updates ship regularly, and a lot of what ships comes straight from what clubs ask for, so if something about your setup is clunky, telling us is the fastest route to changing it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="help">If you get stuck, we are one email away</h2>
      <p>You will not be on your own with this. If a member asks something you cannot answer, or something in the app looks wrong or is missing, email <a href="mailto:support@epegboard.com">support@epegboard.com</a> - we normally reply within 24 hours. And because a lot of what we build comes straight from these messages, flagging a rough edge is also the fastest way to get it fixed or added.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>The short version</h4>
        <p>Tie the switch to a real problem, explain it before the night, keep the tablet accessible, and after each session share the recap leading with the player's own stats. Run that loop every week and your club ends up on its own accounts - not just your tablet.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:14px"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://play.epegboard.com/signup" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sign up free</a> &nbsp; <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://play.epegboard.com/instant-demo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">See it in action</a></p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      @foreach (var f in faqPairs)
      {
        <h3>@f.Q</h3>
        <p>@f.A</p>
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

